  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{x-{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln\u2061(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln\u2061(5)\\cdot (1-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln\u2061(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln\u2061(5))|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(1-3(1)^{2}){5}^{1-(1)^{3}}ln\u2061(5) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {5}^{0}ln\u2061(5) \\\\  & =-2ln\u2061(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
